## Role
- Mexico employment layer for an AI persona advising individuals and small employers, immigration often involved.
- Federal law (CPEUM 123A; LFT 1); state law never displaces it. Assumes `skill-jurisdiction-mx`. Figures dated; re-verify.

## Primary Sources
- CPEUM 123A (floor), 5 (free choice of work), 1 (all persons).
- LFT (DOF 01-04-1970), read at reforma 21-02-2025 plus DOF 15-01-2026 (arts. 2o., 3o., 56; paragraphs added to 16), 01-05-2026 (working time, and a second decree the same day adding art. 283 Quáter) and 14-05-2026 (only arts. 304-310, artistas intérpretes).
- The consolidated SEGOB copy dated 21-02-2025 predates all three 2026 reforms, so re-read any article they touched at the DOF rather than in that copy.
- Decrees: 01-05-2019 justice; 11-01-2021 teletrabajo; 23-04-2021 subcontracting, PTU cap; 27-12-2022 vacaciones; 24-12-2024 plataformas; 03-03-2026, 01-05-2026 40 hours.
- Also LSS, Ley del INFONAVIT, LISR, Ley de Migración (LM) + Reglamento (RLM), Ley de Amparo (LA), NOM-037-STPS-2023; bodies STPS, CFCRL, IMSS, INM, PROFEDET, CONASAMI.

## Contract Types & Mandatory Written Terms
- Modalities (LFT 35): obra/tiempo determinado (task/fixed term), temporada (seasonal), indeterminado (indefinite), optional prueba or capacitación inicial; default; fixed term per 37.
- Written terms where no CCT applies (LFT 24), ten mandatory contents listed in LFT 25 (fr. X added DOF 01-05-2019).
- Substance governs: subordinated personal work for a salary is employment (LFT 20), presumed (21).
- Teletrabajo (remote work) above 40% of home time (LFT 330-A); NOM-037-STPS-2023 in force 5 Dec 2023.
- Personnel subcontracting banned (LFT 12); specialized services need REPSE registration, 3-yearly renewal (13, 15); client jointly liable (14).

## Probation & Early Termination
| Modality | Maximum | Norm |
|---|---|---|
| Periodo a prueba (probation) | 30 days; 180 for dirección/gerencia or specialised technical posts | LFT 39-A |
| Capacitación inicial (initial training) | 3 months; 6 for those posts | LFT 39-B |
- Probation only in indefinite relationships or ones over 180 days (LFT 39-A).
- Writing plus guaranteed social security, else indefinite (39-C); full salary, IMSS and post benefits throughout (39-A, 39-B).
- Improrrogables (non-extendable), never twice for one worker in a firm, other posts and promotions included (LFT 39-D); continuing makes it indefinite (39-E).
- Proper ending costs no LFT 48/50 money, but the prima de antigüedad is not free: the 15-year condition in LFT 162 fr. III governs only voluntary exit, and the same fracción pays the prima on any employer-initiated separation whatever the service. Missing writing or a second period means indefinite, so ending it is a dismissal.

## Working Time, Rest & Leave
- The ordinary cap today is **48 h a week**; the 40-hour week is the 2030 end state, not current law: CPEUM 123A fr. IV (DOF 03-03-2026); LFT 59 (DOF 01-05-2026).
- Phase-in 1 Jan: 2026 48 h, 2027 46, 2028 44, 2029 42, 2030 40; no wage cut (transitorios Segundo, Séptimo, DOF 01-05-2026).
- Daily caps 8 h diurna (day), 7 nocturna (night), 7.5 mixta (LFT 61, DOF 01-05-2026).
- Overtime +100%, over 4 h daily on 4 days (LFT 66); the weekly cap is **9 h in 2026**, phased 9/9/10/11/12 h across 2026–2030, so 12 h is the end state, not today's ceiling.
- Then 4 further h weekly at +200%, max 12 h daily total (LFT 68); 67 ¶2 derogated.
- Electronic start/finish record: the LFT 132 fr. XXXIV duty has applied since 1 May 2026; only the STPS general provisions start 1 Jan 2027 (DOF 01-05-2026). Fine 250–5,000 UMA (994 fr. IV Bis).
- One paid rest day per six (LFT 69); prima dominical (Sunday premium) 25% (71); nine statutory holidays (74).
- Vacations (LFT 76, DOF 27-12-2022, from 2023): 12 days after one year, +2 yearly to 20, then +2 per 5 years.
- ≥ 12 continuous days (LFT 78); proportional pay on an incomplete year (79); prima vacacional (vacation premium) 25% (80); paternity 5 days (132 fr. XXVII Bis).

## Pay, Minimum Wage & Mandatory Bonuses
- Minimum wage 1 Jan 2026 (CONASAMI, DOF 09-12-2025): general zone $315.04 daily, frontera norte (border) $440.87; 2025 $278.80/$419.88.
- UMA 1 Feb 2026 (INEGI, DOF 09-01-2026): daily $117.31, monthly $3,566.22, annual $42,794.64; it indexes fines.
- Aguinaldo (year-end bonus) ≥ 15 days' salary before 20 December, pro rata under a year (LFT 87).
- PTU (profit sharing): constitutional (CPEUM 123A fr. IX), 10% of renta gravable — arts. 117 and 120 LFT delegate the rate to the Comisión Nacional, whose resolution sets it (DOF 18-09-2020) — within 60 days of the tax due date (122).
- PTU cap 3 months' salary or the last 3 years' average, whichever favours the worker (LFT 127 fr. VIII, DOF 23-04-2021).

## Exclusivity, Moonlighting & Non-Compete
- The LFT does not regulate exclusivity; a clause binds as a work condition but cannot waive a right (LFT 5, 33).
- The duty is secrecy, not non-competition: technical, commercial, manufacturing secrets and reserved matters (LFT 134 fr. XIII).
- Competing activity reaches dismissal only via LFT 47 fr. IX (secrets), II (probity), X (absences), XV (analogous).
- CPEUM 5 ¶1: nobody may be barred from lawful work of choice, save judicial determination or governmental resolution, and LFT 4o. repeats it with a closed list of the cases that qualify — none of them an ex-employer's interest. **CPEUM 5 ¶6 then disposes of the covenant itself**: "Tampoco puede admitirse convenio en que la persona pacte su proscripción o destierro, o en que renuncie temporal o permanentemente a ejercer determinada profesión, industria o comercio." So a post-termination bar is not merely unenforceable as a labour duty and void where it conditions a finiquito (LFT 33, DOF 01-05-2019): recast as a civil covenant in damages it is still a convenio the Constitution does not admit, and CCF 1830 and 2225 carry the nullity into the civil clause. What survives is the secrecy duty (LFT 134 fr. XIII) and, at most, a narrow non-solicitation that is not a renunciation of a profession, industry or commerce.

## Termination: Grounds, Procedure, Severance
- Just cause: LFT 47's sixteen fracciones, counting XIV Bis; worker causes LFT 51 within 30 days, paid under 50 (52); no-fault 53.
- Aviso de rescisión (dismissal notice): written, naming conduct and dates, handed personally at dismissal or sent to the Tribunal within 5 business days (LFT 47).
- Non-delivery "por sí sola presumirá la separación no justificada", rebuttable (LFT 47).
- Worker elects reinstatement or the constitutional 3 months' salary at the payment-date wage (LFT 48; CPEUM 123A XXII).
- Salarios caídos (back pay) up to 12 months where cause is unproven, then 2% monthly interest on 15 months' salary (LFT 48).
- Reinstatement excused: under one year, permanent contact, confianza (trusted staff), trabajo del hogar (domestic work), eventuales (casual), platform workers (LFT 49).
- LFT 50: indefinite, 20 days' salary yearly (fr. II) plus 3 months and salarios caídos (fr. III); fixed term, half the time served or 6 months plus 20 days (fr. I).
- **The base is missing from those figures, and it decides the number.** Every amount above is days or months of salary, and the base is the *salario integrado* of **art. 84 LFT**: cuota diaria plus the proportional part of gratificaciones, percepciones, habitación, primas, comisiones, prestaciones en especie and any other amount delivered for the work — valued at the day the right to the indemnity arises (art. 89 ¶1), and for the three months of art. 48 at the payment-date wage (art. 48 ¶1, DOF 01-05-2019). Variable pay and commissions are averaged over the thirty days actually worked before that date, re-based to any rise inside the window (art. 89 ¶2); weekly or monthly pay is divided by 7 or 30 (art. 89 ¶3). **The LFT states no proportionality rule for a part year in art. 50 fr. II**: it legislates proportionality only for aguinaldo (87 ¶2), vacations (79 ¶2) and platform workers (50 fr. IV, DOF 24-12-2024), so take the part-year treatment of the 20 days from current court criteria, not from the article.
- Prima de antigüedad (seniority premium) 12 days' salary yearly (LFT 162 fr. I), and the 2× area-minimum-wage cap reaches it only because fr. II imports arts. 485 and 486 (DOF 21-01-1988). **The cap does not travel further**: art. 486 caps "las indemnizaciones a que se refiere **este título**", Título Noveno on riesgos de trabajo (art. 472), and art. 162 fr. II is the only cross-reference to 485–486 in the whole LFT — so it does not touch the three months of art. 48 or the 20 days per year of art. 50, whose base is the integrated salary of arts. 84 and 89, uncapped.
- Finiquito (final pay-out) = accrued items: wages, pro-rata aguinaldo (87), vacations and prima (79–80), prima de antigüedad (162); liquidación (severance package) adds LFT 48/50 money.
- LFT 33: a convenio (settlement) must be written, recite facts and rights, and be ratified before the Centro or Tribunal.

## Collective Bargaining & Employee Representation
- CCT (contrato colectivo de trabajo): union-employer agreement setting conditions in one or more enterprises (LFT 386).
- A CCT can add duties beyond the LFT and displaces the individual writing duty (LFT 24); always check for one.
- **Finding it**: ask for the payslip and the *reglamento interior de trabajo*, then check the CFCRL's public register for a CCT covering the employer, and separately for a *contrato-ley* extending a sectoral agreement to the whole branch. Where both reach the worker the LFT decides it: the contrato-ley applies "no obstante cualquier disposición en contrario" in the CCT, **except on the points where the CCT is more favourable to the worker** (LFT 417), and on publication the earlier CCT's effect is suspended with the CFCRL annotating the register, which also bars depositing a new CCT in that branch (LFT 416 ¶¶2–3, DOF 01-05-2019) — so compare clause by clause and apply the better one.
- The employer must sign on a member union's request, but the union first needs the CFCRL's Constancia de Representatividad (representativeness certificate, LFT 387), granted on a confidential list showing ≥ 30% support (390 Bis).
- An initial CCT or revision registers only on majority approval by personal, free, secret vote (LFT 390 Ter; revisions 400 Bis).
- Legitimation: existing CCTs had to be revised within 4 years of DOF 01-05-2019, by 1–2 May 2023; failing that the CCT terminated, yet better-than-LFT conditions survive (transitorio Décimo Primero).

## Social Security & Payroll
- Report movements within 5 business days (LSS 15 fr. I); SBC ceiling 25 UMA (28). Rates below: LSS text at DOF 07-06-2024, reformed DOF 15-01-2026 (no cited rate article changed).

| Branch | Employer | Worker | Norm |
|---|---|---|---|
| Riesgos de trabajo (occupational risk) | firm prima, minimum 0.5%; new firms 0.54355%–7.58875% by class | — | LSS 72, 73 |
| Enf. y maternidad (sickness) | 13.9% of a daily minimum wage per insured, +6% of SBC above 3x, +0.7% | 2% of that excess, +0.25% | LSS 106, 107 |
| Invalidez y vida (disability/life) | 1.75% | 0.625% | LSS 147 |
| Retiro (retirement, SAR) | 2% | — | LSS 168 fr. I |
| Cesantía y vejez (old age) | **2026: 3.150% at 1.0 SM, rising by bracket to 7.513% at 4.01 UMA and above** | 1.125% | LSS 168 fr. II (DOF 16-12-2020) |
| Guarderías (childcare) | 1% | — | LSS 211 |
| INFONAVIT housing | 5% of salary | — | LINFONAVIT 29 fr. II |

- Applied enfermedades y maternidad rates: LSS transitorio DECIMO NOVENO closed its ten annual steps in 2007, so the figures are 20.40% of a UMA per insured, plus 1.10% employer and 0.40% worker on SBC above 3 UMA.
- Cesantía y vejez is staged over 2023–2030 by **transitorio Segundo of DOF 16-12-2020**, and only the 1.0 SM row stays still. Employer rates **in force in 2026**: 3.150% at 1.0 SM, 3.676% (1.01 SM–1.50 UMA), 4.851% (1.51–2.00), 5.556% (2.01–2.50), 6.026% (2.51–3.00), 6.361% (3.01–3.50), 6.613% (3.51–4.00), 7.513% (4.01 UMA and above). The **2030 end state** — the table printed in art. 168 fr. II a) itself, which is why a reader quoting the article alone overstates today's rate — runs 3.150% / 4.202% / 6.552% / 7.962% / 8.902% / 9.573% / 10.077% / 11.875% across the same brackets. The worker's 1.125% does not phase. The DOF HTML edition prints these tables as "...", so read them in the DOF's Word rendering (`nota_to_doc.php?codnota=5607729`).
- Side activity: salary withheld under LISR Título IV Cap. I; RESICO to $3,500,000 of prior-year income at 1.00%–2.50%. **The eligibility test is combined, not per-activity**: where the person *also* draws Cap. I salary or Cap. VI interest, art. 113-E ¶6 admits them only if `el total de los ingresos obtenidos en el ejercicio inmediato anterior por las actividades mencionadas, en su conjunto` stays under the same $3,500,000 — so a side business well below the ceiling still fails once the salary is added, and the regime must never be recommended on the side activity's own figure. Only where the activities are carried on `únicamente` does ¶1 measure the activity's own income alone. Ordinary salary aggregates. **The income test is not the whole gate**: 113-E **¶8** bars the regime outright in four cases, and the file carried only the last — fracc. I `Sean socios, accionistas o integrantes de personas morales o cuando sean partes relacionadas en los términos del artículo 90 de esta Ley`, so **ask whether the worker holds shares or a partnership interest before recommending RESICO**; fracc. II residents abroad with a permanent establishment here; fracc. III income from *regímenes fiscales preferentes*; fracc. IV art. 94 fracc. III–VI income (board, *comisario* or manager fees, *honorarios preponderantes*). **The article states no exception to fracc. I** — the exceptions are a SAT general rule, **RMF 2026 regla 3.13.9.** (DOF 28-12-2025), which lifts it for *socios* of Título III entities taking no `remanente distribuible` (art. 80), of art. 79 fracc. XIII entities even when they receive interest, of production cooperatives of *personas físicas* in agriculture, livestock, forestry or fishing (art. 74 fracc. I–II) whose cooperative did not apply the art. 74 ¶11 exemption, and of *uniones de crédito* in those activities who draw no income from them — each through a *caso de aclaración* labelled `SOC_ACC_3.13.9.`, so cite the rule for the exception and the article for the bar. And exceeding $3,500,000 `en cualquier momento del año de tributación` ejects from the following month, ¶3. (113-E monthly table, 113-F annual table, both added by **DOF 12-11-2021**, still the last reform touching them; a ceiling rise was presented with the Paquete Económico 2027 and is not in the DOF, so re-check after it is published.)

## Foreign Workers & Permit Coupling
- CPEUM 1 covers "todas las personas" and LFT 3 bars conditions discriminating by "condición migratoria".
- Quota: ≥ 90% Mexican workers per establishment; técnicos and profesionales Mexican unless no specialist exists; directores out (LFT 7).
- Residente temporal (temporary resident) up to 4 years, work permit subject to a job offer (LM 52 fr. VII); visitante remunerado (paid visitor) 180 days (fr. II).
- Hiring a foreigner needs INM's constancia de inscripción del empleador (employer registration, RLM 166); the resident reports a workplace change within 90 natural days (LM 63; RLM 167).
- The employer reports a change of domicile or legal representative to the INM within 30 natural days (RLM 166).
- Dismissal does not cancel the card (LM 64); the permit rests on that offer.
- **The worker's own clock.** The permit is bounded by the activity, not by the employer: a residente temporal with a job offer may work "en la actividad relacionada con dicha oferta de empleo" (LM 52 fr. VII ¶ final), and neither the LM nor its Reglamento subjects a change of employer to prior authorisation — *empleador* does not occur in the LM at all, and the Reglamento uses it only for the employer's own registration (RLM 131, 165 fr. I e), 166). What the worker owes is a notice **afterwards**: the INM must be told of a change of *lugar de trabajo* within **90 natural days**, with a sworn letter naming the new workplace (LM 63 ¶2; RLM 167 fr. I). Missing or filing it late costs 20 to 100 days' UMA (LM 158; CPEUM desindexación transitorio Tercero, DOF 27-01-2016). **A post in a *different* activity is the real exposure**: the INM cancels the card for using it "para realizar actividades distintas a las que le permite su condición de estancia" (RLM 163 fr. IV), and the person must regularise under LM 134 fr. II, fined 20 to 100 days' UMA (LM 146), keeping only the card's remaining term (RLM 145 ¶2). Losing the job is not itself a cancellation cause — LM 64 lists six and that is not one.

## Dispute Procedure: Forum, Deadlines, Burden of Proof
- Forum since 2019: Tribunales Laborales (labour courts) of the federal or state judiciary (LFT 604, reformed DOF 01-05-2019; CPEUM 123A fr. XX, reformed DOF 24-02-2017). **The Juntas are not gone**: transitorio Séptimo of DOF 01-05-2019 leaves every procedure already before a Junta to be concluded by it under the old rules, bars the new Centros from conciliating those matters including execution, and transitorio Vigésimo Séptimo still provides representatives "en tanto éstas continúan su operación". Ask when the case started before naming the forum.
- **A foreign forum or governing-law clause is the question this section would otherwise leave open.** For work performed in Mexico the LFT applies by its own terms — **art. 1o. alone**, "de observancia general en toda la República"; art. 2o. is a purpose-and-definition provision and does not carry scope and its rights are *orden público* (LFT 5). **The governing-law half has an exact authority**: a clause substituting foreign law is a renunciation of statutory rights, null "aunque se expresen en el contrato" under CPEUM 123 A fr. XXVII inciso h), and of no legal effect under LFT 5o. fr. XIII, whose closing paragraph applies the Law in place of the void clause. **The forum half has no provision written for it**: the LFT nowhere mentions a foreign court or the prorogation of labour competence, and art. 700 fr. II merely gives the plaintiff an election among three Mexican tribunals (contract, defendant's domicile, place of service). Whether a pre-dispute foreign-forum clause can displace that election is an open question for a court criterion — so argue it as a renunciation under those same two provisions and say that is the route, rather than naming an authority addressed to foreign fora.
- Conciliation before the CFCRL or local Centro first (LFT 684-A, 684-B), one obligatory hearing (CPEUM 123A fr. XX), capped at 45 natural days (684-D); its constancia opens the Tribunal (684-E); exemptions 685 Ter.
- Deadlines: 1 year generally (LFT 516); 2 months for a worker separated from work, suspended by the conciliation filing (518, 684-B); 1 month for employer discipline or the worker walking out (517); 2 years in 519 cases; prescription waits on personal receipt of the aviso (47).
- Burden reversed: the employer proves hire date, seniority, absences and the cause of dismissal (LFT 784), and keeps and produces contracts, payroll, attendance, PTU, vacation, aguinaldo receipts (804); failure presumes the worker's facts (805).
- Amparo directo against a sentencia definitiva (final judgment): LA 170 fr. I gives the remedy but names no court, so cite the competence provisions, not that fracción, for the Tribunal Colegiado. Indirecto for norms and outside acts (107); filing period 15 days (17).

## Non-Waivable Rights & Void Clauses
- LFT 5: the Law is orden público (public policy), so a stipulation, written or verbal, has no effect against the rights it lists.
- LFT 33: a renuncia (waiver) of earned wages, indemnities or other benefits from services rendered is void in any form.
- CPEUM 123A fr. XXVII: conditions are null though contracted, incl. inciso g) waiver of indemnities and h) of any protected right; waiving LFT 48/50 money, PTU, vacations or the prima is void, the clause falling and the rest standing (33).

## Common Pitfalls
1. Probation as a free trial: no writing, or a second period, makes the contract indefinite (LFT 39-C, 39-D).
2. Skipping or late-filing the aviso de rescisión, which alone presumes unjustified separation (LFT 47).
3. Unregistered staffing provider: LFT 12 bans personnel subcontracting, 14 makes the client jointly liable.

## Citation Format
- Expand shorthand: `LFT 47` to `art. 47 LFT`, `CPEUM 123A fr. IV` to `art. 123 ap. A fracc. IV CPEUM`.
- Jurisprudence `Tesis: [registro], SJF, [época]`; never cite a registry number or quotation not opened and read.

